---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title={`About`} description="About this site and its author.">
	<div class="flex min-h-[calc(100dvh-4rem)] flex-col items-center px-6 py-6 md:py-16">
		<main class="w-full max-w-[600px]">
			<p class="mt-5 font-sans text-[15px] leading-[1.8] text-ink-80">
				I have long been fascinated by computers and their inner workings. I have built several
				machines of my own, administered various servers and VPSs, and used Linux since the age of
				about 11. I largely suspect this fascination stems from an interest in how things work and
				why they were designed as they were. Books such as <em>The Computer and the Brain</em> by John
				von Neumann and <em>Computation: Finite and Infinite Machines</em> by Marvin Minsky really developed
				this, in that they raise interesting and lasting questions on the nature of computation itself.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 font-sans text-[15px] leading-[1.8] text-ink-80">
				Beside my studies, I’m a fan of history, particularly the <a
					href="/writing/de-hooch-vermeer"
					class="underline decoration-ink/20 underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink"
					>Dutch Golden Age painters</a
				>, and of art more broadly, with a bias towards French Classicism, Romanticism, and the
				Hague School. My first truely scholarly introduction to the arts was when I was about 14 or
				so, and, as all boys do, I was editing Wikipedia pages and found some specifically
				unsatisfactory pages on Dutch art of the seventeenth century. So displeased I was that I set
				out to majorly improve many articles on such topics, and I did, reading plenty of journal
				articles and monographs along the way. In a similar vain for my appreciation of the visual
				arts, I’m fond of cartography, or any good information graphic for that matter.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 font-sans text-[15px] leading-[1.8] text-ink-80">
				I read mostly the classics, and even more so the works of postwar Japan, such as Mishima,
				Seichō Matsumoto, or Murakami. I’m nowhere near as knowledgeable in <a
					href="/writing/philosophical-credo"
					class="underline decoration-ink/20 underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink"
					>philosophy</a
				> as I’d like to be, but I’ve read Sartre, Nietzsche, Voltaire, Mill, Hume, and the like.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-5 font-sans text-[15px] leading-[1.8] text-ink-80">
				I care about <a
					href="/writing/rent-control"
					class="underline decoration-ink/20 underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:decoration-ink"
					>politics</a
				>, too, because I thorougly despise systems that work against the people they are meant to
				serve.
			</p>
		</main>
		<figure class="mt-10 w-full max-w-[600px]">
			<img
				src="https://iiif.micr.io/JpMwf/full/2400,/0/default.webp"
				alt="Still Life with Books, by Jan Davidsz. de Heem"
				width="720"
				height="461"
				class="aspect-[5784/3703] h-auto w-full rounded-sm"
			/>
			<figcaption class="mt-5 text-center font-sans text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink-80">
				Jan Davidsz. de Heem – Still Life with Books, c. 1628
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<div class="w-full max-w-[600px]">
			<Footer />
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>
